[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-22", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 22 — four short reads join the mix: 8 of 29 operations at 2941 / 5118 ops/s", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-22.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 22 - four short reads join the mix (M1 begins): 8 of 29 operations at 2941 ops/s on 8 slots, 5118 on 32\n\n> Seed: iteration 21's S3 - short reads IS1-IS7 toward M1, the whole\n> workload. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2; rung SF1 subset; origin\n> :cross-host, path :lan (levi -> simeon); impl :datalog.\n\n## Generate\n\n  What the subset can serve exactly: IS2 (10 most recent messages by a\n  person with root post and author), IS4 (a message's date and content),\n  IS5 (its creator), IS7 (its replies with the knows flag). IS1, IS3 and\n  IS6 need attributes the subset does not load (profile fields,\n  friendship dates, forums) and are NOT served -- a widened mix, not a\n  complete one.\n\n  Each read written twice, Datalog (IS2 through the ordered drive) and\n  the index-path twin; the startup gate widened to 787 parameters\n  (392 IC + 99 IS2 persons + 99 messages x IS4/5/7). Short-read message\n  ids come from the subset itself (every 353rd of the 35,000 most recent\n  posts, bench/ldbc_short_params.cljk) -- LDBC's driver derives them from\n  complex-read results and this subset has its own population.\n\n  Prediction: short reads at 1-2 ms served; the 8-op mix at the same\n  slots at least as fast as the 4-op mix (the short reads are cheaper\n  than the complex-read mean).\n\n## Rank\n\n  Gate: 787/787 agree; control: an IS5 twin with first/last name swapped\n  -> 40 disagreements, index path disabled.\n\n    mix    slots  rate  on-time%  thru/s   ic02  ic07  ic08  ic09   is2  is4  is5  is7\n    8-op     8    3072  100.00   2940.7    3.3   2.0   1.6   5.6   2.8  1.6  1.6  1.7   (4-op: 2047)\n    8-op     8    4096   13.90   2939.7\n    8-op    32    5120  100.00   5118.4    4.4   2.8   2.3   8.0   4.1  2.3  2.3  2.3   (4-op: 3453)\n    8-op    32    6144   56.57   5631.2\n\n  Ratio to the audited No.1: 47 at 8 slots, 27 at 32. Empty share\n  0.43 -> 0.33.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Held. The mix widened at higher throughput on the same slots, which\n    the growth rule counts. The SUT's per-op budget (~2.3 ms of server\n    time) goes further when half the operations are point lookups.\n  - The four IS reads are gated by two independent implementations\n    agreeing, not by the Neo4j pairing the benchmark names as the\n    reference -- the Cypher twins and the paired run are the next step,\n    and until then these rows say so.\n  - The ceiling moved with the mix (~3,500 -> ~5,600 delivered): the\n    engine is not what bounds the short reads; HTTP and EDN are.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: GROWTH by widening (8 of 29\noperations at >= the 4-op throughput on both slot columns). M1 stands at\n8/29 served, 0 with updates.\n\nSeeds for iteration 23, in order:\n\n  - S12 (reference): Cypher IS2/IS4/IS5/IS7 in ldbc-snb-bench and the\n    paired Neo4j run over the widened set, so the IS rows carry the\n    named reference.\n  - S13 (subset attributes): load person profile fields, friendship\n    dates and forums so IS1/IS3/IS6 (and later IC01/IC03...) have their\n    data; each adds quads and a footprint number for M3.\n  - S11 (per-op cost): server threads 10 vs 8; EDN printing; the drive's\n    independent half as sets.\n  - M2 (updates): the 8 inserts against a transacting SUT -- the design\n    step, not yet an iteration.\n"}]
